<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Josephus</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">37–c. 100</span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century">1st century</a> · Also known as Flavius Josephus, Titus Flavius Josephus</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Flavius Josephus was a Jewish historian and a former commander in the revolt against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>. Captured in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galilee">Galilee</a> in 67 CE, he won the favour of Vespasian and wrote in Rome The Jewish War and Jewish Antiquities, histories of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> from the creation to the revolt of 66–73. His works are the main source for Herodian Judea and its rulers.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/josephus.jpg" alt="Josephus" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Scan by NYPL · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Joseph,_son_of_Gorian,_called_Flavius_Josephus_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Josephus was born Yosef ben Mattityahu in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>, into one of the city's elite families. His father Matthias was a priest of the order of Jehoiarib, the first of the 24 orders of priests in the Temple, and his mother claimed descent from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hasmonean-dynasty">Hasmonean dynasty</a>. At about 26 he travelled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> to negotiate the release of Jewish priests imprisoned by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nero">Nero</a>, and with help from Nero's wife Poppaea he succeeded. On his return, at the outbreak of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-jewish-roman-war">First Jewish–Roman War</a>, he was appointed military governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galilee">Galilee</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In Galilee Josephus fortified towns and contended with a rival, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-of-gischala">John of Gischala</a>. He resisted the Roman army at the siege of Yodfat, or Jotapata, until it fell in 67. By his own account he was trapped in a cave with forty companions, who drew lots to kill one another, and he was one of two left who surrendered. He claimed to have predicted that Vespasian would become emperor, and Vespasian freed him in 69, whereupon he took the emperor's family name, Flavius. To many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> his claims seemed self-serving.</p><p>He served Vespasian's son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/titus-saint">Titus</a> as a translator during the siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> in 70, which ended in the near-total razing of the city and the destruction of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/temple-in-jerusalem">Second Temple</a>. In 71 he went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in Titus's entourage and became a Roman citizen and a client of the Flavian dynasty, with a pension. He wrote all his known works there. He read the destruction of the Temple as a sign that God had turned to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> because of Jewish sins.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>His main works are The Jewish War, of about 75, Antiquities of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a>, of about 94, a history of the world from a Jewish perspective, and his autobiography, the Life. Outside the Scriptures they are the chief source for Second Temple Judaea, with accounts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hasmonean-dynasty">Hasmonean dynasty</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herod-the-great">Herod the Great</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sadducees">Sadducees</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharisees">Pharisees</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/essenes">Essenes</a>, and of figures such as Pontius Pilate, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-the-baptist">John the Baptist</a> and James, the brother of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a>. He is the first known source for the siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/masada">Masada</a>. Historians treat him with caution, since Carl Ritter held in the 1840s that his wish to bring his people honour among <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> filled his history with distortions and exaggerations.</p><p>Christian readers prized him from the 4th century as an independent witness to the world of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a>. Rabbinic writing almost never names him, and he was often seen as a traitor, until Kalman Schulman's Hebrew translation of 1863 revived Jewish interest. William Whiston's English translation of 1732 was among the books Christians most often owned after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bible">Bible</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Josephus” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its wheat was shipped to Rome through the state grain supply, the annona, and <b>Josephus</b>, writing in the first century, says that Africa fed the city for eight months of the year.</blockquote><a href="/empire/africa-proconsularis">Africa Proconsularis · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>They appear in Greek and Latin sources in the first century CE, when Roman writers such as Seneca, Lucan and the Jewish historian <b>Josephus</b> begin to mention them as a new and strong power between the Don and the Caucasus.</blockquote><a href="/empire/alans">Alans · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Josephus</b> describes Tigranes' marching host with its camels, donkeys and mules for baggage, its sheep, cattle and goats for food and its hoards of gold and silver, 'like locusts or the dust of the earth', though the numbers Israelite historians gave were probably exaggerated.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-armenia-artaxiad">Kingdom of Armenia (Artaxiad) · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>North Africa had been one of the richest parts of the Roman world: <b>Josephus</b> wrote that it fed Rome for eight months of the year, and it was an important source of taxes as well as grain and oil.</blockquote><a href="/empire/vandal-kingdom">Vandal Kingdom · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Most of what is known of him comes from the Gospels of the New Testament, written in the decades after his death, with brief references in Roman and Jewish writers such as Tacitus and <b>Josephus</b>.</blockquote><a href="/religion/christianity">Christianity · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>The Tyrian annals that the Jewish historian <b>Josephus</b> quoted from Menander of Ephesus say that he enlarged the island city and rebuilt its temples.</blockquote><a href="/culture/phoenicians">Phoenicians · Society</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Jerusalem, in what is now <a href="/countries/palestine">Palestine</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=31.78,35.23&amp;year=77" aria-label="Jerusalem on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5458 1007 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Josephus"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5908 1277h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5908 1277h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=31.78,35.23&amp;year=77">What was here in 77 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Yet shall I suit my language to the passions I am under, as to the affairs I describe, and must be allowed to indulge some lamentations upon the miseries undergone by my own country.&#8221;</p><footer>The Wars of the Jews, preface &#183; tr. William Whiston, 1737 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/2850/pg2850-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;It is a brave thing to die in war; but so that it be according to the law of war, by the hand of conquerors.&#8221;</p><footer>To his companions in the cave at Jotapata, 67 (The Jewish War, book 3) &#183; tr. William Whiston, 1737 &#183; his own account; they had resolved to kill themselves rather than surrender &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/2850/pg2850-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Now he is equally a coward who will not die when he is obliged to die, and he who will die when he is not obliged so to do.&#8221;</p><footer>To his companions in the cave at Jotapata, 67 (The Jewish War, book 3) &#183; tr. William Whiston, 1737 &#183; his own account &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/2850/pg2850-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><details class="enc-q-more"><summary>3 more</summary><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;The bodies of all men are indeed mortal, and are created out of corruptible matter; but the soul is ever immortal, and is a portion of the divinity that inhabits our bodies.&#8221;</p><footer>To his companions in the cave at Jotapata, 67 (The Jewish War, book 3) &#183; tr. William Whiston, 1737 &#183; his own account &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/2850/pg2850-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Dost thou send me to Nero? For why? Are Nero's successors till they come to thee still alive? Thou, O Vespasian, art Caesar and emperor, thou, and this thy son.&#8221;</p><footer>To Vespasian, as a prisoner, 67 (The Jewish War, book 3) &#183; tr. William Whiston, 1737 &#183; his own account of the prophecy he made; Vespasian became emperor two years later &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/2850/pg2850-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;…it is never dishonorable to repent, and amend what hath been done amiss, even at the last extremity.&#8221;</p><footer>To John of Gischala and the defenders of Jerusalem, 70 (The Jewish War, book 6) &#183; tr. William Whiston, 1737 &#183; his own account &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/2850/pg2850-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote></details><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 540 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
